  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I didn't open the dashboard in Kibana, so I haven't seen the panels render. I also haven't watched the rule run.\n\n**Data:** The logs index has a `timestamp` field, a text `response` field with a `response.keyword` version, and `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url` and `referer`. I grouped on the `.keyword` versions of the text fields.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-10b8e3a7 Web logs overview\" has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days. It's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-10b8e3a7-web-logs-overview-b7448ab21f1a.\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Responses by status code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes by file extension (bar chart)\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-10b8e3a7 5xx spike\" (id `62c061e8-0dbc-4fc7-b68e-8258dd69dd23`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It looks at `kibana_sample_data_logs` over the last 5 minutes. It matches `response.keyword` values from 500 to 599 and fires when the count is above 10. Kibana creates rules disabled, so I enabled it. It has no action attached, so when it fires it only shows up as an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.\n\nThe sample logs are older timestamps, so the rule probably won't fire on them unless new 5xx data arrives.",
